Let me push this further: try even more skip_connect dominance, perhaps all skip except keeping some convs for feature extraction. Let me try a config with skips on most edges but keep 3x3 convs on the final aggregation edges.

Action: evaluate_config {"1<-0": "skip_connect", "2<-0": "skip_connect", "2<-1": "skip_connect", "3<-0": "skip_connect", "3<-1": "nor_conv_3x3", "3<-2": "nor_conv_3x3"}

Thought: This config (0.893587) is worse than config 2 (0.905880). So having 2<-1 as skip_connect hurt performance compared to having it as nor_conv_3x3. The winning pattern so far is config 2: skips on all "from node 0" edges (1<-0, 2<-0, 3<-0) and nor_conv_3x3 on the internal/later edges (2<-1, 3<-1, 3<-2).

Let me refine around config 2. I have ~10872s left, enough for maybe 1-2 more evaluations. Let me try a small variation: change 3<-2 to nor_conv_1x1 or keep 3x3 but change 3<-1 to 1x1. Actually, config 2 had all three non-input edges as nor_conv_3x3. Maybe try replacing one 3x3 with 1x1 to see if it helps. Let me try 3<-2 as nor_conv_1x1 (cheaper, sometimes better).

Action: evaluate_config {"1<-0": "skip_connect", "2<-0": "skip_connect", "2<-1": "nor_conv_3x3", "3<-0": "skip_connect", "3<-1": "nor_conv_3x3", "3<-2": "nor_conv_1x1"}

Thought: Config 4 gave 0.902680, slightly worse than config 2 (0.905880). So config 2 remains the best. With only 3487s left, I likely can't afford another full evaluation (they cost 6000-9000s). I should finalize with the best configuration found: config 2.

